    "posts": [
      {
        "id": 85129,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-12-28T02:50:27.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Silverleaf\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/48/2332_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Silverleaf:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Well as a general rule for everyone in the world, when people around you start making annoyed/upset noises that's the point where you should probably <em>shut the hell up</em>, or at least calm it down a bit.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Calming down would be a good idea, were I ever entirely capable of it (I've been trying, seriously; stopping and thinking before I blurt stuff out is totally my New Years' Resolution, and I'm even starting early).&nbsp; But the shut up part…er, no.&nbsp; Did Robespierre shut up when the King and Queen of France disapproved of what he said?&nbsp; Did Martin Luther shut up about the problems of medieval Catholicism?&nbsp; No, men of good conscience <strong>always</strong> say what they feel needs to be said.&nbsp; It'd be really nice if they weren't punished for that, they certainly don't want to be - but they have to be willing to risk it, or nothing ever changes for the better (and changing for the worse is an absolute inevitability of the universe we live in).&nbsp; I would rather be disliked for speaking up, than be silent party to a unilateral error of omission which could end up costing the whole of society.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It's pretty arrogant to assume that someone who knows a reasonable amount about a subject hasn't already heard your type of opinion before.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why would I assume anything of the sort?&nbsp; All human beings are unique; no person's capacity to contribute should ever be taken for granted.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Your opinion is not a gift that we should all be grateful for.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is only <em>your</em> opinion.&nbsp; Which I value about as much as you do mine.&nbsp; I did not listen when people tried to tell my child-self that I was not special, and I'm not about to start now.&nbsp; People deserve better than to have their contributions marginalized in such a fashion.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I can listen to as many people tell me how homeopathy cures cancer as there are stars in the sky and it doesn't change the fact that I think (backed up by a whole lot of science and a whole <em>lack</em> of science on their part) that they are wrong.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So?&nbsp; How are they hurting you by saying such things?&nbsp; As long as you don't follow their misguided advice (or even if you do, so long as you do so responsibly and with full understanding of the possible costs and consequences, weighed against the potential benefits and your confidence-or-lack-thereof in them), why should they not be free to say whatever they please in a forum of public discourse?&nbsp; They can say homeopathy works, you can say it doesn't, and the whole world is better off from the debate being aired, rather than suppressed.&nbsp; Maybe, just maybe, there's a tiny part of homeopathy that works, and people need to be exposed to the idea in order to eventually cut away the chaff and discover the true secret within it.</p>\n<p>And similarly, I feel that there's a part of statistics which <strong>doesn't</strong> work as well as it's almost invariably assumed to, and that people should be discouraged from overvaluing it.&nbsp; If someone says \"cars are 99.999% certain not to spontaneously explode\", I feel the need to point out that this statement <em>in no way</em> prevents <em>your</em> car from spontaneously exploding, and that you should take whatever steps you feel are appropriate to protect yourself against the danger, however small, that you might be the lucky loser of that particular lottery.&nbsp; The decision is yours to make, but there's no reason why other people shouldn't be allowed to influence it, as long as they don't force you to agree with them.&nbsp; If all of society accepted homeopathy without question but considered statistics to be the worst form of quackery, you and I would be on exactly the opposite sides here, but I'd like to think I'd at least give you the time of day, in view of how passionately you argued in favor of your \"logic\" and \"evidence\", even if I didn't agree with you that those things were important.</p>",
        "post_number": 71,
