      {
        "id": 85140,
        "name": "Gislef",
        "username": "Gislef",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/g/4af34b/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-09T20:11:22.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Well, having played Skinwalker twice now, with selected decks rather than random or theme, we'd have to rank Skinwalker at a high 3/low 4.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>For one thing, it seems like he has more minions/Targets than any other villain (21, and the Vast Followings tend to bring them back). If not, it's real close. That means you're always busy shooting something: voodoo pins or relics or cursed acolyte or Ophidia. Other than maybe the zombies and sometimes the pins, you can't really afford to let anything stay out.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Granted, we used Rook City for the first environment, and Silver Gulch for the second (random draw on the first, choice on the second), and they both beat us up pretty badly. Especially when Skinwalker flipped and chewed through an Explosives Wagon. But it seems like you need mass-attacking (Ex-Pat, Tempest) and damage boosting (Legacy) to get by. Don't worry much about deck manipulation and fancy-dancy juggling, and just concentraet on hard-hitting heroes.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>It takes us forever to flip him. By then we've cleared the board pretty much of all of his minions, and fortunately Legacy in our 2nd game drew Leads... and Next Evolution pretty early, letting him negate Skinwalker's damage. That's the only thing that kept us going down much earlier. Once we flipped him, he pretty much went through us with whatever other minions he drew.</p>",
        "post_number": 82,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:08:43.188Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
